WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.203
Load balancing multiple local LLM servers can improve total request throughput, availability,

00:00:05.203 --> 00:00:06.478
and workflow flexibility.

00:00:06.478 --> 00:00:12.432
It gives tools one stable endpoint while backend servers can be added, removed, repaired,

00:00:12.432 --> 00:00:13.282
or resized.

00:00:13.282 --> 00:00:15.408
The durable expectation is simple:

00:00:15.408 --> 00:00:19.235
ordinary load balancing routes requests across independently running endpoints;

00:00:19.235 --> 00:00:24.338
it does not automatically combine machines into one faster single inference session.

00:00:24.338 --> 00:00:27.740
A reliable setup depends on understanding each backend.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:31.992
Hardware resources, model files, runtime settings, context length, API compatibility,

00:00:31.992 --> 00:00:35.394
streaming behavior, and failure modes all shape capacity.

00:00:35.394 --> 00:00:37.520
Routing should reflect that reality.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:42.197
Round-robin may be enough for similar servers, while weighted, least-connections, health-check,

00:00:42.197 --> 00:00:46.450
model-aware, queue-based, and failover strategies become important as workloads diverge.

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:49.001
Workload awareness keeps the service usable.

00:00:49.001 --> 00:00:52.828
Interactive chat, coding agents, batch summarization, document analysis, embeddings,

00:00:52.828 --> 00:00:57.080
long-context requests, and tool-using workflows have different latency, state, retry,

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:58.356
and concurrency needs.

00:00:58.356 --> 00:01:02.608
Sticky sessions may matter when state or cache behavior matters.

00:01:02.608 --> 00:01:06.435
Stateless requests may route freely to any compatible backend.

00:01:06.435 --> 00:01:08.986
Operations and security complete the design.

00:01:08.986 --> 00:01:14.940
Measure real workloads, monitor the gateway and every backend, watch queue growth and failures,

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:19.617
preserve streaming behavior, and troubleshoot mismatches in model names, runtime versions,

00:01:19.617 --> 00:01:20.893
endpoints, and timeouts.

00:01:20.893 --> 00:01:26.421
Protect local LLM endpoints because they may process sensitive prompts, documents, source code,

00:01:26.421 --> 00:01:27.696
and tool outputs.

00:01:27.696 --> 00:01:32.799
The goal is a practical local inference service that is measured, reliable,

00:01:32.799 --> 00:01:34.500
and safe to use.
